            <ArticleTitle>An unusual intracranial metallic foreign bodies and panhypopituitarism.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>33</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.11604/pamj.2014.19.33.3862</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 49 years old man, with a history of aggression at the age of 18 years by a pair of scissors, who consulted for unilateral migraine headaches look straight. Paraclinical explorations concluded that trauma to anterior pituitary by a metallic foreign body from the right nostril to the sella, responsible for panhypopituitarism and sinusitis. The headaches are frequent causes of consultation, often treated symptomatically but rarely explored. The direct trauma to the pituitary gland, by a metallic foreign body, is exceptional. We report the case of neglected panhypopituitarism, discovered 31 years after injury with a pair of scissors.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
